-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merksha Žarković was a Serbian nobleman who ruled the Principality of Valona (Vlorë) in southern Albania from about 1396 until his death in 1414.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/merkurios"><span class="enc-name">Merkurios</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merkurios was a king of Makuria in Nubia around the end of the 7th and the beginning of the 8th century; an inscription implies that his reign began in 697.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Mermnad dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mermnad dynasty ruled the Kingdom of Lydia from Sardis from about 680 BCE until the Persian conquest around 547 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merneptah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merneptah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merneptah was the fourth king of the 19th Dynasty of Egypt, son and successor of Ramesses II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merneptah-stele.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merneptah Stele</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Merneptah Stele is an inscribed granite slab of the Egyptian pharaoh Merneptah, set up around 1208 BCE and found at Thebes in 1896.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/merobaudes-general"><span class="enc-name">Merobaudes (general)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Flavius Merobaudes was a Roman general, probably of Frankish origin, who served the emperors Valentinian I and Gratian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/merobaudes-poet"><span class="enc-name">Merobaudes (poet)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Flavius Merobaudes was a Latin poet and Roman general of the 5th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merodach-baladan-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merodach-baladan-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merodach-baladan II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merodach-baladan II (Marduk-apla-iddina II) was a Chaldean chief of Bit-Yakin who seized the throne of Babylon in 721 BCE with Elamite support and held it until Sargon…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/meroe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/meroe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Meroë</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Meroe was an ancient city on the east bank of the Nile in present-day Sudan, the capital of the Kushite Empire from around the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merogaisus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merogaisus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merogaisus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merogaisus was a Frankish king who, together with Ascaric, broke the peace with Rome on the lower Rhine around 306.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/meroitic-script"><span class="enc-name">Meroitic script</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Meroitic script was the writing system of the Kingdom of Kush in its Meroitic period, used from about the second century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merovech"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merovech.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merovech</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Merovech was a semi-legendary king of the Salian Franks in the mid-5th century, from whom the Merovingian dynasty took its name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merovingian-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merovingian dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Merovingian dynasty was the first royal house of the Franks, ruling from the fifth century until 751.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merowe-sudan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merowe-sudan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merowe, Sudan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Merowe is a town on the Nile in northern Sudan, near the fourth cataract and the ancient sites of Napata and Jebel Barkal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merrimack-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merrimack-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merrimack River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Merrimack River rises in New Hampshire and flows about 190 km south and then east through Massachusetts to the Atlantic at Newburyport.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mers-el-kebir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mers El Kébir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mers El Kébir is a port town on the Algerian coast a few kilometres west of Oran, with one of the best natural harbours of North Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mersa-gawasis"><span class="enc-name">Mersa Gawasis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mersa Gawasis is an archaeological site on the Red Sea coast of Egypt, south of Safaga, where a harbour was used by Middle Kingdom expeditions to Punt and other lands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merseburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merseburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merseburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Merseburg is a town on the Saale river in Saxony-Anhalt, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/merseburg-charms"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/merseburg-charms.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Merseburg Charms</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Merseburg Charms are two magic spells in Old High German, written down in the 9th or 10th century in a manuscript of the cathedral library of Merseburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mersin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mersin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mersin</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mersin is a port city on the Mediterranean coast of southern Turkey, capital of Mersin province, which covers the western part of ancient Cilicia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mertola"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mertola.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mértola</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mértola is a town in the Alentejo region of southern Portugal, on a hill above the Gua